      "level": "easy",
      "template_selector": null,
      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186179644,
          "id": "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30",
          "title": "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](flux-074b7ecff299cc0383ef72e5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric similar ellipses. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nThe field points into the boundary everywhere. Its outward normal component is negative, giving net inflow.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](flux-15b47ada5da79a241e0a99e5.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is negative. **true**.\n",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/key": "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive.",
              ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
