    {
      "level": "hard",
      "template_selector": null,
      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186179644,
          "id": "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30",
          "title": "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](flux-1d254c2d6e221e63cd6ad4b6.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric circles. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nEvery boundary vector of $\\mathbf V$ is tangent to the boundary, hence perpendicular to the outward normal. Thus $\\mathbf V\\cdot\\mathbf n=0$ at every point, and its flux is zero.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](flux-a7bb45526ffb643a645aada9.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is not positive. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
